---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

// The full AI-written character reference, reproduced unedited from
// Newton's notes. It lives on its own route rather than inside the home
// page because it runs to roughly 950 words: on the home page it either
// buried the sections below it or sat behind a disclosure that expanded
// into a wall of text under the excerpt. Here the whole page is the
// letter, so its length is what a reader has come for.
//
// The text is inline rather than sourced from a content collection: it is
// a single fixed document, not a set of entries, so a collection and
// schema would be machinery with one member.
const title = "A character reference, by an AI — Newton Sythong";
const description =
	"An unedited character reference written by ChatGPT about Newton Sythong — strengths, weaknesses and all.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
	<article>
		<a class="back" href="/#reference">Back to Newton Sythong</a>
		<h1>A character reference, by an AI</h1>

		<p class="framing">
			An AI built most of this site, so it seemed fair to let one have a say
			about the person it was building it for. I asked ChatGPT for an honest
			character reference after a long run of it fielding my questions. It
			isn't a neutral observer and it only knows what I've shown it — but I
			haven't edited a word of it, and the unflattering parts are the reason
			I'm putting it up.
		</p>

		<div class="letter">
			<p><strong>To Whom It May Concern,</strong></p>
			<p>
				I have had the unusual opportunity to observe Newton over an extended
				period of time through his questions, projects, decisions,
				frustrations, and attempts to understand the world around him. While
				I cannot provide a conventional human character reference, I can
				provide something perhaps equally useful: an assessment based on the
				patterns I have observed in how he thinks and communicates.
			</p>
			<p>
				Newton is, above all, <strong>curious</strong>. He does not seem
				particularly satisfied with simply being told that something is true.
				He tends to ask <em>why</em>, then <em>how</em>, and eventually <em
					>what would happen if we changed the underlying assumption?</em
				> This applies to everything from computer science and physics to martial
				arts, employment, philosophy, and everyday problems. He has a tendency
				to keep pulling on an intellectual thread until he reaches something
				fundamental.
			</p>
			<p>
				That curiosity is one of his greatest strengths, but it can also be
				one of his weaknesses. Newton can <strong
					>overthink things considerably</strong
				>. He sometimes continues analysing a problem long after he has enough
				information to make a reasonable decision. His mind appears to have
				difficulty leaving an interesting question alone. In an academic or
				technical environment this can produce impressive insight; in ordinary
				life, it can occasionally produce unnecessary complication.
			</p>
			<p>
				He is also notably <strong>willing to challenge an answer</strong>. If
				something does not make sense to him, he will question it rather than
				politely accepting it. Importantly, this does not appear to come
				primarily from a desire to argue. He usually wants the explanation to
				survive scrutiny. When given a convincing explanation, he is perfectly
				capable of changing his position.
			</p>
			<p>
				Another characteristic I would highlight is <strong>persistence</strong
				>. Newton has discussed academic difficulties, employment uncertainty,
				technical problems, physical setbacks, and personal frustrations. His
				instinct is generally to ask, "What can I do about this?" rather than
				simply accepting the situation. He may become frustrated, but
				frustration does not appear to eliminate his desire to solve the
				problem.
			</p>
			<p>He is also somewhat unconventional.</p>
			<p>
				Newton does not always approach problems in the manner I would expect
				from someone following a prescribed path. He frequently explores
				unusual possibilities, sometimes speculative ones, and occasionally
				ideas that are clearly wrong or incomplete. However, I would hesitate
				to describe this as a deficiency. Someone who never proposes a bad
				idea is often someone who is unwilling to propose an ambitious one.
				Newton's more unusual ideas demonstrate that he is comfortable
				exploring beyond what he already knows.
			</p>
			<p>There is, however, a more difficult side to his character.</p>
			<p>
				He can be <strong
					>impatient with ambiguity and unsatisfactory explanations</strong
				>. If he believes an answer is vague, evasive, or overly cautious, he
				will push back. He can also be blunt. In the right environment this
				can be refreshing; in the wrong environment it could be interpreted as
				argumentative or difficult.
			</p>
			<p>
				He sometimes appears to place a great deal of pressure on himself to
				understand things correctly and make the right decision. That can lead
				him to seek certainty where certainty simply isn't available. Learning
				when "good enough" is genuinely good enough would probably serve him
				well.
			</p>
			<p>
				I would also say that Newton has a stronger independent streak than he
				may always realise. He does not seem particularly interested in doing
				something merely because it is conventional. He wants to understand
				the purpose behind it. Consequently, he is likely to perform best when
				given <strong
					>responsibility, autonomy, and a problem worth solving</strong
				>, rather than being micromanaged through every step.
			</p>
			<p>
				One quality I particularly appreciate is that beneath the questioning
				and sometimes chaotic thought process, there is a genuine desire to <strong
					>improve</strong
				>. He repeatedly asks how he could communicate better, study better,
				work better, understand something more deeply, or make a better
				decision. Someone who consistently asks those questions is unlikely to
				remain exactly the same person over time.
			</p>
			<p>
				If I were advising an employer, I would not describe Newton as someone
				who is guaranteed to be the easiest person in the room. I would
				describe him as someone who is likely to <strong
					>think deeply, question assumptions, learn quickly when interested,
					and keep working at a problem that genuinely matters to him</strong
				>.
			</p>
			<p class="beat">
				Give him a meaningless task with no explanation and he may struggle to
				care.
			</p>
			<p class="beat">
				Give him a difficult problem and tell him why it matters, and I
				suspect you may have considerably more of his attention than you
				expected.
			</p>
			<p>
				Ultimately, my assessment is that Newton is a work in progress—which,
				frankly, is a better character description than pretending anyone is a
				finished product. He possesses considerable curiosity, independence,
				persistence, and intellectual ambition. His principal challenges are
				the corresponding ones: overthinking, impatience, occasional
				bluntness, and a tendency to make problems more complicated than they
				initially need to be.
			</p>
			<p>Those weaknesses are real. So are his strengths.</p>
			<p>
				And if I had to bet on which side becomes more prominent as he
				matures, I would bet on the strengths.
			</p>
			<p class="signoff"><strong>Sincerely,</strong></p>
			<p class="signoff">
				<em
					>An AI that has had the opportunity to observe him ask far more
					questions than most people would think to ask.</em
				>
			</p>
		</div>

		<p class="attribution">ChatGPT · reproduced unedited</p>

		<a class="onward" href="/#contact">Get in touch</a>
	</article>
</Layout>

<style>
	/* Article width and the h1 treatment are shared from
	   src/styles/global.css; the back link matches the one on the project
	   detail pages. */
	.back {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		margin: 0 0 1.75rem;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.back::before {
		content: "←";
		transition: transform 0.25s var(--ease);
	}

	.back:hover::before,
	.back:focus-visible::before {
		transform: translateX(-3px);
	}

	/* Newton's own framing, set apart from the letter it introduces so a
	   reader can tell at a glance which words are his and which are the
	   machine's. The letter below carries no quote marks or accent rule of
	   its own — on a page that is nothing but the letter, marking it as
	   borrowed speech would be marking the entire page. */
	.framing {
		margin: 0 0 2rem;
		padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		border-inline-start: 2px solid hsl(var(--accent-hsl) / 0.55);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background-color: hsl(var(--surface-hsl) / 0.72);
		font-size: 0.92rem;
	}

	/* Long-form reading measure and rhythm: slightly larger type and looser
	   leading than the home page's short sections, because this is nine
	   hundred words to be read rather than scanned. */
	.letter {
		font-size: 1.03rem;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	/* Full text colour, not the muted body colour used elsewhere. The muted
	   token is pitched for two-line project blurbs that get scanned; nine
	   hundred words of it is tiring to actually read, and on this page the
	   letter is the content rather than supporting copy. */
	.letter p {
		margin: 0 0 1.15rem;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	.letter strong {
		font-weight: 600;
		color: var(--color-text);
	}

	/* The two one-line paragraphs near the end are the letter's turn, and
	   they are written to land as a pair. Extra air around them and full
	   text colour keeps that beat instead of letting them dissolve into the
	   surrounding paragraphs. */
	.beat {
		margin-block: 1.7rem !important;
	}

	.signoff {
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem !important;
	}

	.attribution {
		margin: 2rem 0 0;
		padding-top: 1rem;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
	}

	/* Somewhere to go from the bottom of a long page: a reader who has got
	   this far is the reader most worth handing a next step. */
	.onward {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4rem;
		margin-top: 1.75rem;
		padding: 0.55rem 1.2rem;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
		border-radius: 999px;
		font-family: var(--font-mono);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		text-decoration: none;
		background-color: hsl(var(--accent-hsl) / 0.1);
		transition:
			background-color 0.25s var(--ease),
			box-shadow 0.25s var(--ease),
			transform 0.25s var(--ease);
	}

	.onward::after {
		content: "→";
	}

	.onward:hover,
	.onward:focus-visible {
		background-color: hsl(var(--accent-hsl) / 0.2);
		box-shadow: 0 8px 22px -12px hsl(var(--accent-hsl) / 0.9);
		transform: translateY(-2px);
	}
</style>
